1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,032
MyCampusView Academy.

2
00:00:03,082 --> 00:00:06,697
AI for Young Builders, video 3.

3
00:00:06,747 --> 00:00:09,770
Data: features and labels.

4
00:00:10,950 --> 00:00:15,487
An AI is only as good as the examples it learns from.

5
00:00:15,537 --> 00:00:19,900
So let us look closely at those examples: the data.

6
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:23,868
Here is some data about fruit.

7
00:00:23,918 --> 00:00:26,002
Each row is one example.

8
00:00:26,052 --> 00:00:31,159
Colour, size and shape are features: clues we can measure.

9
00:00:31,209 --> 00:00:36,850
The fruit name is the label: the answer we want the AI to learn.

10
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:40,973
Good features connect to the label.

11
00:00:41,023 --> 00:00:44,502
Colour is a great clue for apple or orange.

12
00:00:44,552 --> 00:00:47,457
Ear shape helps tell cats from dogs.

13
00:00:47,507 --> 00:00:52,054
But the date a photo was taken tells you nothing at all,

14
00:00:52,104 --> 00:00:54,270
so it is a useless feature.

15
00:00:55,650 --> 00:00:57,582
Watch out for this trap.

16
00:00:57,632 --> 00:01:01,960
If every dog photo shows a brown dog on grass, the AI

17
00:01:02,010 --> 00:01:06,090
might learn that a brown thing on grass means dog.

18
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:10,220
Then it gets a white dog indoors completely wrong.

19
00:01:11,550 --> 00:01:14,419
So good data has three things.

20
00:01:14,469 --> 00:01:16,072
Lots of examples.

21
00:01:16,122 --> 00:01:23,466
Lots of variety: different colours, sizes, angles, lighting and backgrounds.

22
00:01:23,516 --> 00:01:26,190
And labels that are correct.

23
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:29,061
Let us recap.

24
00:01:29,111 --> 00:01:31,884
Data is a set of examples.

25
00:01:31,934 --> 00:01:37,095
Features are the clues; the label is the answer.

26
00:01:37,145 --> 00:01:42,306
More examples, and more variety, make better AI.

27
00:01:42,356 --> 00:01:45,780
Next: How computers see: pixels.
